WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.850
MAX SALTONSTALL: With almost
500 sessions at Next 2018,

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:05.430
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:08.100
So if you didn't see how
Google enabled rapid response

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.177
to Meltdown and Spectre,
then stay tuned,

00:00:10.177 --> 00:00:11.260
because here's the Rewind.

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:15.180
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.460
Google's cloud and those
global scale traffic

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.070
and tremendous workloads, all
while maintaining security

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.910
for you and your data.

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:27.640
So when big security challenges,
like Spectre and Meltdown,

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.640
come around, our
architecture needs

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.110
to help us respond quickly
and adapt to these new issues.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.860
Having the right
infrastructure design

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:37.950
lets us move quickly to
respond to new threats

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:41.370
and make changes across the
large-scale fleet efficiently.

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.800
In the last year, we had to
address new discoveries that

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.980
impacted the entire industry
appearing at the processor

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:47.550
level.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:50.010
To start, let's dig into the
technology behind the platform

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:52.380
and how that creates
a secure environment.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.960
Google's cloud technologies are
built with defense and depth

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.150
in mind, applying nine
layers of security,

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.640
starting from the hardware,
moving all the way up

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.770
to the individual people
using the platform.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:03.960
For example, Google
loves containers.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.550
And we run all
sorts of workloads,

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:07.980
internal and external,
in them everyday.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.200
This is great for security
because containers provide

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.410
critical safety assurances by
isolating workloads and keeping

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:16.150
them from interacting
in any way.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:18.330
But in some cases, the
limits provided by containers

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:19.770
are too basic.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:22.120
So we've adopted a
two-boundary rule,

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.660
which states any interesting
code must be separated

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:26.730
by two defensive barriers.

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:28.560
That way, any one
new vulnerability

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:31.500
can't break our separation
between virtual machines.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.920
Beyond our VM isolation, we've
improved on existing sandbox

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:37.710
technology with gVisor,
which provides security

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:39.930
through OS-level virtualization.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:42.660
We can reduce the
vulnerability of system calls

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.000
and give us a secondary
sandbox for applications

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.140
running on containers.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.560
This combination of strong
defenses between containers

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.630
and lightweight VM
management allowed

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:54.090
us to quickly update
our entire fleet

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:56.850
after we deployed kernel page
table isolation to combat

00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:00.360
Meltdown and Retpoline to
prevent Spectre's branch target

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:01.590
injection attack.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.390
We were able to get
to a secure state

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:06.210
without noticeable downtime
or performance impact,

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:09.350
all because we could do
live migration of VMs, patch

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:11.340
the underlying
processor and OS code,

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.520
then migrate the VMs back
to repeat this process again

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:15.442
with the next host.

00:02:15.442 --> 00:02:17.400
Google isn't the only
one out there discovering

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.700
new vulnerabilities.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.450
It's key that our security teams
engage with the wider security

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:24.180
community to share
information and fix problems

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.577
so we can all be
safer together online.

00:02:26.577 --> 00:02:28.410
We started our vulnerability
rewards program

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.990
in 2010, which helps benefit
the security research

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.990
community through collaboration
as well as cash bonuses.

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:36.177
Google has paid out
almost $12 million

00:02:36.177 --> 00:02:38.760
as of July of this year, and we
continue to improve and update

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.660
our prize amounts,
including $31,337

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:45.060
for a discovered sandbox escape.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:47.340
Ask me later about that number.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.860
We also think carefully
about the people and process

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.620
of securing our cloud, and
how we approach the monitoring

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.970
and response to a
security incident.

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:57.660
We need a strong cycle of
observe, orient, decide,

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:00.360
and act, with teams that
span multiple disciplines

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.230
and multiple sites
around the globe

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:06.072
so we can keep watch
every hour of every day.

00:03:06.072 --> 00:03:07.530
If you'd like to
see the full talk,

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.370
check out the link in
the description below.

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:11.453
And don't forget to subscribe
to the Cloud YouTube

00:03:11.453 --> 00:03:13.567
channel for more great
